claimed: intent-precedence-rule — Proposal and Request genuinely overlap ("Can we move Thursday sync to Friday?" is both), and the reference calls this the mutually-exclusive-in-practice problem, to be settled by a stated precedence rule rather than by a better classifier. The design question is WHERE such a rule lives. A doc comment cannot be read at runtime and a prompt parameter does not travel with the type, so the answer that matches this library is a typeclass beside the Schema: Taxonomy[I] carrying what the schema cannot say about its own domain. Measure whether stating the tie-break actually helps. NOTED IN ADVANCE, because it decides how the result may be read: the fixture labels encode MY precedence, so a rule matching them should help by construction -- the claim under test is that a taxonomy can carry its tie-breaks and that stating them measurably moves the classifier, NOT that this particular rule is the right one.
